<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">André Rigaud</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1761">1761</a>–<a href="/years/1811">1811</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">André Rigaud was a leader of the free people of colour in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a>. He fought the British occupation in the 1790s but lost the War of the Knives against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toussaint-louverture">Toussaint Louverture</a> in 1799–1800 and went into exile. Returning in 1810, he set up the State of South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> in opposition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandre-petion">Alexandre Pétion</a> and died the next year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andre-rigaud.jpg" alt="André Rigaud" width="320" height="459" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andr%C3%A9_Rigaud.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Benoit Joseph André Rigaud was born on 17 January 1761 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/les-cayes">Les Cayes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>, the son of André Rigaud, a wealthy French planter, and Rose Bossy Depa, an enslaved woman. His father acknowledged him at a young age and sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a>, where he trained as a goldsmith. On his return to the colony he entered politics as a successor to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vincent-oge">Vincent Ogé</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julien-raimond">Julien Raimond</a> in championing the free people of colour, holding to a reading of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/declaration-of-the-rights-of-man-and-of-the-citizen">Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen</a> that gave civil equality to all free people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>By the mid-1790s Rigaud led an army in the Ouest and Sud, with authority to govern from Étienne Polverel, one of the three French civil commissioners. His power rested on the free black and mulatto planters of the South, who feared the masses of former slaves; he refused to ally with leaders such as Romaine-la-Prophétesse and sided with the commissioners who abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in 1793. His soldiers included blacks and whites, and his protégés included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandre-petion">Alexandre Pétion</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-pierre-boyer">Jean-Pierre Boyer</a>, both later presidents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>.</p><p>Although he respected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toussaint-louverture">Toussaint Louverture</a>, his superior in rank, Rigaud would not give up power in the South, and he still held to the colony's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> system that placed mulattoes just below whites. The rivalry, which the Comte d'Hédouville had encouraged, led to the War of the South in June 1799, when Toussaint's army invaded his territory. Defeated, he sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1800, but on 1 October his schooner Diana was captured by the USS Experiment and he was held on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts">Saint Kitts</a> until his release.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Rigaud returned in 1802 with the expedition of General Charles Leclerc, sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> to remove Toussaint and restore French rule and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. After it failed he went back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and was for a time held in the Fort de Joux, where Toussaint died in 1803. He came back a third time in December 1810 and made himself President of the State of the South, against both Pétion and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-christophe">Henri Christophe</a> in the North. Soon after his death in 1811 Pétion recovered the South. His tomb stands on a small hill between Camp-Perrin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/les-cayes">Les Cayes</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “André Rigaud” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The republic was also divided internally when General <b>André Rigaud</b> returned from exile and set up a separate State of South Haiti in 1810; after his death in 1811 the south rejoined the republic in 1812.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti-1806-1849">Republic of Haiti (1806-1849) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Yellow fever and malaria devastated the reinforcements sent from Britain, while Toussaint Louverture, now fighting for the French Republic, and <b>André Rigaud</b> pressed them from the north and south.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-occupation-of-saint-domingue">British occupation of Saint-Domingue</a></li><li><blockquote><b>André Rigaud</b>, who had led the free people of colour in the south during the Haitian Revolution and lost the War of the Knives to Toussaint Louverture in 1800, returned from exile in France in 1810.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-south-haiti">State of South Haiti</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/les-cayes">Les Cayes</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/haiti">Haiti</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/les-cayes">Les Cayes</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/haiti">Haiti</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=18.2,-73.75&amp;year=1793" aria-label="Les Cayes on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2551 1489 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of André Rigaud"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3001 1759h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3001 1759h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=18.2,-73.75&amp;year=1793">What was here in 1793? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
